LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Stock Settings: the warehouse constitution", "14", "<p>The final module of the final foundation track, and it opens where its siblings did: the settings screen, read as a constitution — because every default here is a policy the whole stock system will enforce silently, thousands of times, forever. The walk, field by consequential field, with the owner and the accountant in the room:</p><ul><li><b>Allow negative stock</b> — module 5's sternest conviction, now as configuration: OFF as default and discipline, ON only for the bounded, eyes-open exceptions (the go-live window, the deliberate fast-trade model with same-day posting) — and never, ever, flipped to silence an error message, because the message IS the control. The setting's review belongs to the annual walk; its emergency toggle belongs to nobody.</li><li><b>Default valuation method</b> — the most consequential default in the stock system (module 5's words): the accountant's policy, chosen with the whiteboard conversation, applied consistently, changed only as a formal accounting event. The field encodes the decision; the walk is where the decision is confirmed to still be the decision.</li><li><b>UOM and conversion enforcement</b> — the settings that make Buying 2's arithmetic structural: conversions validated at entry, the unit disciplines enforced where configuration allows, because the factor-of-twelve disease (module 4's count signatures) is cheapest to refuse at the field level.</li><li><b>Auto-creation toggles</b> — the material-request automation switches (Procurement 3's machinery has its master switches here), batch and serial naming series (the identity system's registrar — series chosen once, at setup, with the same decade view as every naming decision).</li><li><b>The tolerance links</b> — over-receipt and over-delivery allowances' global fallbacks (the gate numbers of both trading tracks, with their per-item overrides riding above).</li><li><b>Stock frozen up to</b> — the period lock, important enough to own the next chapter whole.</li></ul><p><b>The walk's discipline.</b> As in both trading tracks: each field read as the policy question it is, the answers written into the implementation record, the annual re-walk on the calendar (the firm changed this year; did its constitution keep up?) — and the corridor toggle convicted one final time: a setting that keeps obstructing honest work is RETUNED in the walk, deliberately, with the owner; it is never flipped in the hallway to make one afternoon easier, because the hallway flip repeals the constitution for everyone to answer one letter. Three tracks have now taught this in three vocabularies; the consultant who holds it has a career's worth of prevented disasters that will never appear on any invoice.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The freeze: period discipline for stock", "13", "<p>The accounting close taught both money tracks that closed is closed; this chapter is the stock system's version — the machinery that makes the past stop moving.</p><p><b>Stock frozen up to.</b> The setting names a date; stock entries dated on or before it are locked — no new postings into the frozen past, no edits to its truth — except by the ROLE the companion setting names (the narrow door: the accountant or controller who may, deliberately, post into frozen periods when the close's own corrections require it). The mechanics are simple; the discipline they enable is the point: module 5 showed how backdated entries recompute everything forward — valuations re-blend, COGS revises, reported figures move — and the freeze is how the firm draws the line behind which that may no longer happen casually: <b>reported truth stays reported</b>.</p><p><b>The monthly rhythm.</b> The freeze advances with the close, as a ritual with an order: the month's stragglers swept IN first (the late paperwork posted with honest dates — the batched, announced sweep of module 5's governance, run deliberately before the door shuts); the reconciliations run (the stock-to-accounts bridge agreeing — module 5's certificate — plus the month's standing audits: the stock-entry review, the unallocated reads); THEN the frozen date advances to the month's end, and the period is closed in both systems at once — stock and accounts freezing together, because a stock period open behind a closed accounting period is an invitation for the two to diverge, and the bridge's whole job is that they never do. The close that runs this order takes an hour; the close that freezes first and sweeps after generates its own exceptions.</p><p><b>The narrow door's governance.</b> The frozen-edit role is held by few (two names is a healthy number), its use is EVENTFUL (a posting into a frozen period carries its reason and its accountant-awareness — the module 5 boundary: closed-period entries are accounting decisions, not warehouse conveniences), and its usage is READ (the quarterly look at what went through the door, because a narrow door used weekly is a wide door with paperwork). The pattern to prevent is the freeze theatre: the date set once at go-live and never advanced (the lock that locks nothing), or advanced but with the edit role granted broadly (the lock with keys under the mat) — both leaving the past as editable as ever while the setting claims otherwise, which is worse than no freeze because it manufactures false assurance. The freeze that works is boring: advances monthly, guards absolutely, opens rarely, and logs when it does — the profile of every control this academy has built.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Item lifecycle governance: birth, change & retirement", "13", "<p>Modules 1-7 assumed items exist and behave; this chapter governs their LIVES — because the item master is the stock system's genome, and ungoverned item creation is how a clean implementation becomes a jungle in eighteen months.</p><p><b>Birth: the creation gate.</b> Who may create items is a PERMISSION decision made deliberately: narrow (the controller, the master-data owner) for most firms, because every new item is a permanent commitment — a code, a UOM architecture, identity flags with their ten-year consequences (module 3), planning fields, tax templates — and the casually-created item gets all of these wrong at once. The gate's disciplines: the <b>duplicate check</b> first, always (search by every name the thing might wear — the jungle's dominant species is the same product under three codes, splitting its stock, its history, and its reorder logic three ways: the duplicate is not untidy, it is three lying bins wearing one product's face); the <b>template discipline</b> (item groups carrying defaults — new items born from their group's template inherit the architecture instead of improvising it); and the naming convention held (module 1's decade rule, item edition).</p><p><b>Change: amendment rights.</b> The item's structural fields — UOM conversions, identity flags, valuation-relevant settings — are guarded harder than its descriptive ones, because structural changes on an item WITH history are events (the conversion changed under existing stock re-reads every quantity; the identity flag flipped is module 3's managed cutover, not an edit). The permission layer separates the two: descriptions and images broadly editable; structure behind the narrow door with the same eventfulness as frozen-period posting.</p><p><b>Retirement: the delisting flow.</b> Items die too — the pruning quadrant (module 7) executed — and the flow is a sequence, not a click: open documents closed (no live orders or requests referencing it), stock cleared through the ladder's honest rungs (the item disabled with stock stranded is a bin nobody can act on), THEN disabled — out of every dropdown and search, history intact forever (never deleted: the ledger's testimony includes retired witnesses). The annual range review (module 7's pruning) feeds this flow its candidates; the flow's discipline is what keeps the assortment a decision instead of an accumulation.</p><p><b>Variants, briefly and honestly.</b> Where products differ only by attribute — size, colour, strength — the variant machinery (one template, many variants) beats separate free-standing items: shared architecture, coherent reporting, one place to govern. The boundary judgment the consultant owns: genuine attribute families are variants; genuinely different products are items; and the wrong choice in either direction costs (variants stretched over different products entangle them; separate items for one product's sizes rebuild the duplicate jungle deliberately). The full variant machinery is its own study; this track's requirement is recognising the decision and making it at architecture time — the item chapter's ten-year view, one last time.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Quality integration: the inspection layer", "13", "<p>Quality has threaded through three tracks — the receiving fork, the pencil-whipped checklist, the batch quality notes. This chapter assembles the machinery into its structural form: inspections as configured, binding process.</p><p><b>The template.</b> Per item (or item group), the <b>Quality Inspection Template</b> names the checks: parameters, acceptable ranges or criteria, reading types. The template is the quality standard MADE OPERATIONAL — the difference between <i>check it properly</i> (a wish) and a named list a person completes and signs (a control). Templates are written with whoever owns the standard (the pharmacist superintendent, the QA lead, the regulator's checklist transcribed) and versioned as standards change.</p><p><b>The binding points.</b> Inspections attach where the flags say: <b>at purchase receipt</b> (the gate — Procurement 6's machinery: goods flagged inspection-required cannot be received into accepted stock without a completed inspection; the fork's evidence document); <b>in-process</b> (where repack or light production warrants a mid-flow check — the strips inspected before they join sellable stock); and <b>at delivery</b> (the outbound check for trades where what leaves must be verified — the serialized equipment powered on before dispatch, the export order's documented pre-shipment check). Each binding is proportionate by design: the flags sit on the items whose stakes warrant them (pharma actives yes, stationery no — the module 3 economics, quality edition), and the binding is REAL: where the flag is on, the document flow refuses to proceed without the inspection — the structural version of the discipline, which is the whole point of configuring it rather than requesting it.</p><p><b>The honest inspection.</b> The pencil-whip conviction, final form: an inspection performed at the desk without the goods opened manufactures evidence of diligence not done — worse than no layer, because it converts YOUR OWN quality file into perjury. The defences are the audit family's: inspections spot-checked against reality (the QA lead re-opening a sampled carton against its filed inspection), inspection times read (the checklist completed in forty seconds was not performed), and the inspector's accountability framed as module 1 framed every custody — the signed inspection protects the honest inspector the day the batch goes wrong, because their diligence is on file.</p><p><b>The quality calendar and its reads.</b> Quality's rhythms join the structure: inspection results trended (failure rates by supplier feeding the scorecard — Procurement 8's thread; by item feeding the spec conversations), quarantine's weekly review (the standing ritual, three tracks old), the disposal compliance file maintained (module 3's governed endings), and the annual template review (standards drift; checklists must follow). Quality integrated this way is not a department; it is a set of binding points in the same document flows everything else uses — which is exactly why it works at an 11-person firm: no quality team required, just flags, templates, and the calendar's eye.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Roles & the adjust permission: separating stock's authorities", "14", "<p>Both trading tracks separated their money authorities; stock's authorities are subtler and the stakes are the same. This chapter is the separation design — and the permission that deserves the module's sternest paragraph.</p><p><b>The four authorities.</b> Across this track, four distinct powers have moved stock's truth: <b>MOVE</b> — transfers and stock entries (custody changing hands: the network's daily verbs); <b>ADJUST</b> — the reconciliation (truth OVERRIDDEN by count: the dialogue's final word); <b>VALUE</b> — rate-asserting documents (Material Receipts with typed rates, revaluations: money created or moved by assertion); and <b>CONFIGURE</b> — the settings, the item structure, the tree (the constitution itself). Each is a different kind of trust, and the design principle is the familiar one: the authorities CHECK each other only if they are separated — the person who moves stock daily should not casually hold the power to adjust the record their movements produce, and nobody's routine role includes rewriting the constitution.</p><p><b>The adjust permission — the eraser's license.</b> Module 4 built the discipline (investigate, then adjust, in the open); this chapter builds its enforcement: the reconciliation permission is <b>the most dangerous grant in the stock system</b>, because it is the one document that overrides the ledger's computed truth with an asserted number — every other document ADDS truth; this one REPLACES it. Held broadly, it dissolves every control upstream: the custodian who can adjust their own custody can erase their own differences (the count's fairness dead), the shrinkage number reads whatever the adjusters left it (module 4's managed number, unmanaged), and the investigation discipline becomes optional the day everyone holds the eraser. The design: adjust held by TWO names (the controller and one senior backstop), never by warehouse operators, never by branch managers for their own branches (the self-adjustment ban is absolute — the custody and its correction never share a hand), with value thresholds routing large adjustments to a second sign-off even among the two. Every adjustment carries its investigation reference (module 4's file), and the monthly adjustments read — by author, value, warehouse, reason — is the permission's audit.</p><p><b>The rest of the map.</b> MOVE: broad by necessity (the network runs on transfers) but shaped by module 1's warehouse restrictions (branch users to their leaves; van users to their vans) — the wrong choice made impossible, not just un-defaulted. VALUE: narrow (rate-asserting documents behind thresholds — modules 2 and 5's convictions as permissions). CONFIGURE: narrowest (settings, item structure, tree changes — the constitution's editors named, and few). And the sizing honesty, three tracks old: eleven people means hats share heads, but the irreducible core holds — the adjuster is not adjusting their own custody, the configurer's changes are eventful, and the two-names rule for the eraser survives every org chart, because it is two PEOPLE, not two departments.</p><p><b>Permissions enforce the design; they do not substitute for one.</b> The closing principle from every structural module: the role matrix is the design made binding — and the design conversation (who SHOULD hold what, and why) is the deliverable; the matrix is its encoding. A permissions setup nobody can explain is a design nobody made.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The stock calendar: every rhythm, one page", "13", "<p>Eight modules installed rhythms; the consolidation, as ever, is the operating system. The stock function's recurring work, one page, with owners — the third and final calendar of the academy.</p><p><b>Daily.</b> The exceptions scan: negative flags (counted same-day — module 5's drill), unclosed runs from yesterday (module 6), identity exceptions queued (module 3); the cycle counts (the day's lines, fifteen minutes — module 4); the van tailgates (evening reconciliation, every van, every day — module 6's smallest gate); and the bins read at the desks that trade on them.</p><p><b>Weekly.</b> The transit review (runs closed, the room breathing, aging at zero — modules 2 and 6); the quarantine review (contents, reasons, next steps — three tracks' standing ritual); the expiry horizon (the worklist walked, ladder rungs assigned — module 3); the network dashboard (one page at the centre — module 6); and the branch weekly pages pushed out.</p><p><b>Monthly.</b> The health page compiled, read with the MD, ambers owned (module 7); the stock-to-accounts bridge agreed (module 5); the stock-entry audit (purpose, value, author — module 2's boring page); the shrinkage and adjustments reads (module 4; Chapter 5's permission audit); the straggler sweep and the freeze advance (Chapter 2's ordered ritual); and the FEFO/override and identity exception reads (module 3).</p><p><b>Quarterly.</b> The ABC and matrix refresh (module 7); the branch scorecards and reviews (module 6); the dead-stock cause read to procurement (module 7's letter); the allocation and lateral logs read (module 6's letters); the frozen-door usage read (Chapter 2); and the planning-fields exception review with buying (the joint ritual both tracks share).</p><p><b>Annually.</b> The full count (module 4's production); the mock recall (module 3's drill, timed and filed); the tree review (module 1 — rooms without twins hunted both directions); the settings walk (Chapter 1); the range review feeding the delisting flow (Chapter 3); the quality template review (Chapter 4); and the calendar itself re-read — rhythms that earned their keep confirmed, decorations retired honestly.</p><p><b>The page's law, final statement.</b> A rhythm without a name is a hope; the deliverable is this page WITH the names — sized to the firm (the eleven-person company's page has four names appearing often; the hundred-branch network's has a department), reviewed when people change, and treated as what it is: the stock function's entire operating system on one sheet. The consultant who leaves configured software has done half the job; three tracks have now said it, and the third saying is the summary: <b>the calendar with owners IS the implementation</b> — everything else is preparation for it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The handover test: custody that survives its custodian", "13", "<p>The academy's deepest thread surfaces one last time: every structural module of every track has been building toward the same test, and stock — the function most often trapped in one trusted person's head — needs it most.</p><p><b>The test, stock edition.</b> Could a competent newcomer, given what exists, run the warehouse function for a month with ZERO questions answerable only by the person who left? Walk what <i>what exists</i> must mean, module by module: the TREE tells them where stock can be and who answers for each room (1); the DOCUMENTS tell them how it moves and every movement's why (2); the IDENTITY system tells them what everything is, when it expires, and how to run the letter that might come (3 — the run-book on the shelf, drilled and timed); the COUNTING programme tells them what to verify and when, with the investigation discipline written (4); the VALUATION setup tells them what everything costs and forbids the shortcuts (5); the NETWORK playbook tells them the runs, the policy, the tailgates (6); the HEALTH page tells them what good looks like and where this month is amber (7); and THIS module's calendar tells them what to do every day, week, month, quarter — with the settings guarding the constitution while they learn. Every NO in the walk is a module incompletely done — the test is the audit, and the audit is the goal.</p><p><b>The storekeeper's path.</b> The handover test read from the other side is a career ladder, and the consultant who frames it this way changes implementations: the CUSTODIAN (module 1's framing — answerable for a room, protected by documents, confirmed by counts) grows into the CONTROLLER (the adjust permission's holder, the calendar's owner, the health page's compiler) precisely BY the system existing — because a structured function has legible seniority (the clean-close streak, the accuracy rate, the owned rhythms are a promotion case written in numbers), while the unstructured one promotes whoever the owner trusts, which is a ceiling for everyone including the owner. The training deliverable that serves this: each role's slice of the calendar as their run-book, the disciplines taught as their protection (three tracks of that framing), and the path named — people keep disciplines that visibly lead somewhere.</p><p><b>The documentation set.</b> The handover test passes on artifacts, and this track's are now enumerable: the opening file (module 4 — how the truth began), the tree and defaults record (1), the policy pages (allocation, thresholds, dead-stock definition, short-dating ladder — every written number the modules demanded), the run-books (recall, negative-stock drill, count production, van cycle), the calendar with names (6), and the settings record with its walk history (1-2). None of these is created FOR the handover; every one is a by-product of doing the modules properly — which is the academy's quiet thesis, stated once more: <b>the system that runs well and the system that hands over well are the same system</b>, and the founder-bottleneck (the concern that began these tracks' structural chapters) is solved not by finding a hero to trust but by building the structure a competent person can hold. The three-week absence, attempted deliberately before circumstance imposes it, is the test run in production — and the case study runs it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: the structural year — and the graduate", "16", "<p>The final case study, in two acts: HealthTrade's stock function becomes structure, and the certification's graduate shows what three tracks buy.</p><p><b>Act one — the structural year.</b> <b>Q1, the constitution (Ch. 1-2):</b> the settings walk with the MD and accountant — allow-negative confirmed OFF (the go-live window long closed), the method confirmed and recorded, frozen-upto instituted on the monthly rhythm with the ordered ritual (sweep, reconcile, advance), the narrow door held by the accountant and the ops lead. <b>Q2, the incident and the narrowing (Ch. 5):</b> the adjustments read flags a pattern — a branch supervisor with the reconciliation permission adjusting their own small variances, uninvestigated; nothing sinister found on review, but the design flaw is the finding: the eraser was distributed. The permission narrows to two names network-wide, the self-adjustment ban is written, and the next quarter's shrinkage read becomes honest for the first time (it RISES slightly — the differences previously erased now surface, investigate, and trend — the number getting worse because the truth got better, explained to the MD in exactly those words). <b>Q3, item governance (Ch. 3):</b> the creation gate catches a duplicate at birth — a new supplier's brand name for a paracetamol formulation already coded under its generic — merged before it splits a single bin; the annual range review feeds 60 delistings through the retirement flow, each cleared then disabled, history intact. <b>Q4, the absence test (Ch. 7):</b> the founder's three weeks abroad — the trading tracks passed their functions last year; this year the WAREHOUSE runs on its calendar, its run-books, and its two-name controls: twenty-one days, one incident (a transit shortfall, worked by the protocol, resolved from documents), zero founder-only questions. The handover test, passed by the function that used to live entirely in one storekeeper's memory.</p><p><b>Act two — the graduate (the academy's closing argument).</b> A consultant holding all three certifications walks a prospect's warehouse — the distressed distributor from module 5's vignette — and reads it in a day, because every symptom now names its module: the flat sixty-warehouse tree (Inv 1), receipts at supplier-like rates (Inv 2's side door), batch fields empty on pharma lines (Inv 3 — the recall that cannot run), no count since the audit two years ago (Inv 4), allow-negative on since week two (Inv 5), branches buying retail from a network whose runs slip weekly (Inv 6 — and the diagnostic mercy: the branches are escaping, not rebelling), no page anyone reads (Inv 7), and every permission held by everyone (Inv 8). The findings deck writes itself in the modules' order; the remediation quote prices in the sequence module 5 taught; and the engagement it wins is delivered by the same curriculum run forward. That consultant — able to see a trading firm whole, from the supplier's master record to the branch's tailgate arithmetic to the naira of margin on the year's page — is what 24 modules, 216 lessons, and three certifications exist to produce. The academy's three tracks close where they began: with the conviction that a business runs on documents, masters, and rhythms — and that the professional who commands all three can walk into any trading firm in Nigeria and leave it structurally better than they found it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Patterns, the eighth law & the track complete", "14", "<p>The structural layer's scar tissue, then the law that closes the track — and the academy's foundation.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the corridor toggle.</b> Symptom: allow-negative on since an error message annoyed someone; frozen-upto never advanced; tolerances changed per complaint. Disease: the constitution edited casually (Ch. 1-2). Fix: settings behind the narrowest door, the annual walk as the only editing room, the corridor answered with the override paths every control provides.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the distributed eraser.</b> Symptom: everyone adjusts; shrinkage reads suspiciously clean; the same custodies never vary. Disease: the reconciliation permission held broadly, self-adjustment unbanned (Ch. 5) — every upstream control dissolved by the one that overrides them. Fix: two names, the ban written, thresholds inside even the two, and the monthly adjustments read as the permission's standing audit — expecting shrinkage to RISE when the fix lands, and briefing the owner that worse numbers mean truer ones.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the duplicate jungle.</b> Symptom: one product, three codes; stock, history, and reorder logic split three ways; nobody can say what the firm holds of anything. Disease: ungoverned creation (Ch. 3). Fix: the gate narrowed, the duplicate check mandatory, the templates carrying architecture, and the existing jungle merged deliberately — item by item, stock consolidated by document, the survivors' history preserved.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the freeze theatre.</b> Symptom: the frozen date set at go-live and never moved, or moved with edit keys under every mat. Disease: false assurance manufactured (Ch. 2) — the past as editable as ever while the setting claims otherwise. Fix: the monthly advance in the close's ordered ritual, the narrow door with its usage read, and the theatre named for what it is.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — quality theatre.</b> Symptom: inspections filed in forty seconds; checklists perfect forever; the batch that fails in the market with a clean inspection on file. Disease: the pencil-whip at structural scale (Ch. 4) — the firm's own quality file converted to perjury. Fix: spot-checks against reality, inspection-time reads, and the inspector's protection framing that makes honesty the self-interested choice.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the unowned calendar.</b> Symptom: rhythms that ran while the consultant watched and stopped when they left. Disease: the page without names (Ch. 6) — the implementation's second half never delivered. Fix: the calendar with owners as the closing deliverable, reviewed at every staffing change — and the follow-up visit that checks the rhythms, not the software.</p><p><b>The eighth law.</b> The tree is the map of custody; stock moves only by documents; identity is operational; the count is a dialogue; valuation is written at the moment of movement; the network is one stock in many rooms; stock health is read, not felt. The eighth closes the track as its siblings closed theirs, because it is the same truth in its third and final vocabulary: <b>custody that lives in structure — masters, documents, rhythms, and separated authorities — survives growth, turnover, and absence; custody that lives in a trusted head survives exactly as long as the head stays.</b> The settings are the constitution, the freeze is the past's guardian, the adjust permission is the eraser licensed to two, the calendar is the heartbeat, and the handover test is the audit of all of it.</p><p><b>The track — and the foundation — complete.</b> Eight modules: the map (1), the movements (2), the identities (3), the count (4), the money (5), the network (6), the reading (7), the structure (8). And with it the academy's third certification closes the foundation triad: <b>Selling</b> (money in), <b>Procurement</b> (money out), <b>Inventory Management</b> (the goods both of them move) — 24 modules, 24 laws, three continuous case studies, and one repeated conviction wearing every vocabulary the trade owns: documents over memory, masters over improvisation, rhythms over heroics, structure over trust. Take the assessment; then go read a warehouse.</p>"),
]

